You are working on the ExamFlow project.

We are now starting:

PHASE 5 — SEATING ASSIGNMENT

The goal of this phase is to assign every student registered for an exam
to a specific examination room and physical seat.

This phase connects:

Exam
  ↓
Student Roster

with:

Exam
  ↓
Selected Rooms
  ↓
Usable Seats

The output is:

Student
  ↓
Exam Room
  ↓
Physical Seat

This is one of the most important business workflows in ExamFlow.

IMPORTANT:

Keep the implementation deterministic, explainable, auditable, and simple.

Do NOT use AI or machine learning.

Do NOT implement Crowdmark integration.

Do NOT generate personalized exam PDFs.

Do NOT generate QR codes.

Do NOT generate signature lists yet.

Do NOT generate seating maps yet.

Those belong to later phases.

==================================================
1. READ PROJECT DOCUMENTATION
==================================================

Before coding, read:

- PRD.md
- ARCHITECTURE.md
- DATABASE.md
- SECURITY.md
- DEVELOPMENT.md
- AGENTS.md

Also inspect the complete implementation of:

- Phase 1 — Authentication
- Phase 2 — Course + Exam
- Phase 3 — Student + Roster
- Phase 4 — Building + Room + Seat

Follow existing architecture and naming conventions.

Do not introduce a new architecture.

Before coding, provide a concise implementation plan.

==================================================
2. PHASE 5 SCOPE
==================================================

Implement:

1. Seat assignment model
2. Assignment generation
3. Deterministic assignment algorithm
4. Room ordering
5. Seat ordering
6. Student ordering
7. Assignment preview
8. Assignment confirmation
9. Assignment validation
10. Capacity checking
11. Assignment regeneration
12. Assignment viewing
13. Assignment search
14. Assignment statistics
15. Authorization
16. Audit logging
17. Database migration
18. Backend APIs
19. Frontend UI
20. Automated tests

Do NOT implement:

- Crowdmark
- QR codes
- personalized PDFs
- signature sheets
- seating maps
- exam printing
- AI
- external scheduling systems

==================================================
3. CORE CONCEPT
==================================================

Create a separate exam-specific assignment entity.

Conceptually:

Exam
  │
  ├── Student
  │
  └── Seat Assignment
          │
          ├── Room
          └── Seat

The physical Seat must remain independent from the student.

Do NOT put:

student_id

inside the Seat table.

==================================================
4. ASSIGNMENT MODEL
==================================================

Create an ExamSeatAssignment entity.

Suggested fields:

id
exam_id
student_id
room_id
seat_id
assignment_order
created_at
updated_at

Follow DATABASE.md and project naming conventions.

The exact fields may differ if a better existing design exists.

==================================================
5. DATABASE RELATIONSHIPS
==================================================

An assignment must reference:

exam
student
room
seat

All references must use foreign keys.

The assignment must represent:

Student X
taking Exam Y
in Room Z
at Seat A01

==================================================
6. UNIQUE ASSIGNMENT
==================================================

A student can have at most one seat assignment for a given exam.

Database constraint:

unique(exam_id, student_id)

A physical seat can be assigned to at most one student for a given exam.

Database constraint:

unique(exam_id, seat_id)

These constraints are mandatory.

Do not rely only on application-level validation.

==================================================
7. ROOM CONSISTENCY
==================================================

The assigned seat must actually belong to the assigned room.

For example:

Assignment:

exam = CSC108 Final
room = IB101
seat = A01

must be valid only if:

A01 belongs to IB101.

Do not trust frontend-provided room_id.

Validate this in the backend/database logic.

==================================================
8. EXAM ROOM VALIDATION
==================================================

A room used by an assignment must belong to the exam's selected rooms.

For example:

Exam CSC108 Final

Selected rooms:

IB101
IB102

A student cannot be assigned:

IB103

unless IB103 has first been added to the exam.

==================================================
9. SEAT VALIDATION
==================================================

A seat can be assigned only if:

- seat exists
- seat belongs to selected exam room
- seat is usable
- seat is not already assigned for the exam

An unusable seat must never be assigned.

==================================================
10. STUDENT VALIDATION
==================================================

A student can be assigned only if:

- student exists
- student is in the exam roster
- student is not already assigned

Do not allow assigning arbitrary students who are not registered for the exam.

==================================================
11. CAPACITY CHECK
==================================================

Before generating assignments:

registered_students =
number of students in exam roster

available_seats =
number of usable seats in selected exam rooms

If:

registered_students > available_seats

do not generate assignments.

Return a clear error:

"Insufficient seating capacity."

Example:

Students:
185

Usable seats:
170

Result:

Assignment cannot proceed.

Do not partially assign 170 students.

==================================================
12. CAPACITY WARNING
==================================================

If:

available_seats > registered_students

the system may show:

185 students
220 available seats

Extra seats:
35

This is normal.

Do not require every physical seat to be occupied.

==================================================
13. ASSIGNMENT ALGORITHM
==================================================

Implement a deterministic algorithm.

The first version should be intentionally simple.

Default:

1. Retrieve exam roster.
2. Sort students deterministically.
3. Retrieve selected rooms.
4. Sort rooms deterministically.
5. Retrieve usable seats.
6. Sort seats deterministically.
7. Assign students sequentially.

Example:

Students:

100000001
100000002
100000003
100000004

Rooms:

IB101
IB102

Seats:

IB101:
A01
A02
A03

IB102:
A01
A02
A03

Result:

100000001 → IB101 A01
100000002 → IB101 A02
100000003 → IB101 A03
100000004 → IB102 A01

This is deterministic.

==================================================
14. STUDENT ORDERING
==================================================

Default ordering should be:

student_number ASC

Student number is preferred because it is:

- deterministic
- stable
- easy to audit
- easy to reproduce

Do not sort by display name by default.

Do not use random ordering.

==================================================
15. ROOM ORDERING
==================================================

Use a deterministic room order.

Recommended:

building_code ASC
room_number ASC

For example:

IB101
IB102
IB201
HW100

The exact ordering must be documented.

==================================================
16. SEAT ORDERING
==================================================

Use deterministic physical ordering.

Recommended:

row_label ASC
seat_number ASC

For example:

A01
A02
A03
...
B01
B02
...

Do not depend on database natural order.

==================================================
17. ASSIGNMENT ORDER

Store:

assignment_order

This represents the deterministic sequence used by the assignment algorithm.

Example:

1 → Student 100000001 → IB101 A01
2 → Student 100000002 → IB101 A02
3 → Student 100000003 → IB101 A03

This will later be useful for:

- signature lists
- seating maps
- audit
- reproducibility

==================================================
18. DETERMINISM

Running the same assignment operation twice against the same inputs should
produce the same result.

Given:

same roster
same rooms
same seats
same ordering rules

the output must be identical.

Do NOT use:

random()
UUID ordering
database natural order
timestamps

to determine seating.

==================================================
19. RANDOMIZATION

Do NOT implement randomization in Phase 5.

If randomized seating is required later, implement it as an explicit,
auditable strategy.

The default algorithm must remain deterministic.

==================================================
20. ASSIGNMENT STRATEGY

Design the code so the algorithm can later support multiple strategies.

For example:

SequentialSeatAssignmentStrategy

But do not implement multiple strategies yet.

Do not over-engineer this.

A simple service abstraction is enough.

==================================================
21. ASSIGNMENT GENERATION WORKFLOW

Preferred workflow:

User opens:

Exam → Seating

System displays:

Students:
185

Available seats:
220

Current assignments:
0

Then:

[ Generate Assignment ]

The system calculates a preview.

No database changes yet.

==================================================
22. ASSIGNMENT PREVIEW

Preview should show:

Students:
185

Rooms:
3

Available seats:
220

Assigned:
185

Unused:
35

Then show sample assignment rows.

For example:

Student Number | Name | Building | Room | Seat

100000001 | Alice Student | IB | 101 | A01
100000002 | Bob Student   | IB | 101 | A02

Do not display the entire dataset unnecessarily if the roster is large.

Pagination is acceptable.

==================================================
23. CONFIRMATION

The user must explicitly confirm the generated assignment.

Example:

"Generate seating for 185 students using 220 available seats?"

[Cancel]
[Confirm Assignment]

Do not automatically commit preview results.

==================================================
24. FINAL ASSIGNMENT

When confirmed:

- revalidate roster
- revalidate rooms
- revalidate seats
- revalidate capacity
- regenerate assignment from authoritative database state
- insert transactionally
- commit

Do not trust the preview payload.

==================================================
25. TRANSACTION

Assignment creation must be atomic.

Preferred:

BEGIN

validate all inputs

generate assignments

insert all assignments

COMMIT

If any error occurs:

ROLLBACK

Never leave a partially assigned exam.

==================================================
26. CONCURRENCY

Consider two administrators attempting to generate seating simultaneously.

The system must prevent duplicate assignments.

Use:

- database unique constraints
- transaction isolation appropriate to the application

Do not introduce distributed locking unless absolutely necessary.

If another assignment already exists, return a clear conflict.

==================================================
27. EXISTING ASSIGNMENTS

An exam may already have assignments.

Do NOT silently overwrite them.

If assignments exist:

display:

"Seating has already been generated."

Options:

View Assignment

Regenerate Assignment

Regeneration must require explicit confirmation.

==================================================
28. REGENERATION

Implement a safe regeneration workflow.

Before regeneration:

show:

Current assignments:
185

Regenerating will replace all current seating assignments.

Require explicit confirmation.

Example:

"Regenerate seating?"

[Cancel]
[Regenerate]

==================================================
29. REGENERATION TRANSACTION

Regeneration should be atomic.

Preferred:

BEGIN

delete current assignments for exam

recalculate assignments

insert new assignments

COMMIT

If anything fails:

ROLLBACK

The previous assignments should remain intact if the transaction fails.

==================================================
30. ASSIGNMENT VERSION

If the architecture can support it cleanly, include an assignment version
number.

Example:

version 1
version 2

This is useful for audit and future document generation.

However:

do not create a complex version-control system.

If versioning is not necessary in the current architecture, document it as
a future enhancement.

==================================================
31. ASSIGNMENT STATUS

Keep status simple.

Possible:

DRAFT
CONFIRMED

If the existing architecture does not need this, do not introduce it.

Do not create a large state machine.

==================================================
32. MANUAL ASSIGNMENT

Do NOT implement drag-and-drop manual seating in Phase 5 unless explicitly
required by PRD.md.

The first release should focus on deterministic automatic assignment.

Manual corrections can be considered later.

==================================================
33. SEARCH

Provide assignment search by:

- student number
- student name
- room
- seat

Search must be server-side.

Do not load the entire assignment dataset into the browser.

==================================================
34. ASSIGNMENT LIST

Create an assignment table.

Example:

------------------------------------------------------------
CSC108 Final — Seating Assignment

Student        Student ID   Building   Room   Seat
Alice Student  100000001    IB         101    A01
Bob Student    100000002    IB         101    A02
Charlie Student 100000003   IB         101    A03
------------------------------------------------------------

Provide:

- search
- filtering
- pagination
- sorting where appropriate

==================================================
35. ROOM SUMMARY

Display assignment statistics by room.

Example:

Room       Seats Used    Seats Available

IB101      80            80
IB102      75            80
IB103      30            60

This is useful for administrators.

Do not create advanced analytics.

==================================================
36. ASSIGNMENT SUMMARY

Display:

Registered students:
185

Assigned:
185

Unassigned:
0

Available seats:
220

Unused seats:
35

Rooms used:
3

This should be calculated by the backend.

==================================================
37. UNASSIGNED STUDENTS

If the system encounters any unassigned students after generation:

This is a critical error.

The transaction must fail.

Do not produce a partial assignment.

==================================================
38. DUPLICATE SEATS

If two students would receive the same seat:

This is a critical error.

The transaction must fail.

The database constraint must also prevent it.

==================================================
39. DUPLICATE STUDENTS

If one student would receive two seats:

This is a critical error.

The transaction must fail.

The database constraint must also prevent it.

==================================================
40. ROOM CAPACITY

Do not use:

room.capacity

as the actual seat inventory.

Use:

count(usable seats)

from the selected exam rooms.

Room capacity is a physical/institutional limit.

Actual assignment capacity comes from usable seats.

==================================================
41. SEAT AVAILABILITY

For Phase 5, a seat is available if:

seat.is_usable = true

and:

seat is not already assigned to the exam.

A seat assigned in another exam is NOT automatically unavailable.

Physical seats are reusable across different exam times.

==================================================
42. ROOM SCHEDULING

If the project already supports exam dates/times:

Do not assign the same physical room to overlapping exams.

If Phase 4 implemented room conflict detection, reuse it.

Do not duplicate scheduling logic.

==================================================
43. ROOM VALIDATION

Before assignment:

For every selected exam room:

verify room is active.

For every seat:

verify seat belongs to the selected room.

For every seat:

verify seat is usable.

==================================================
44. AUTHORIZATION

Use Phase 1 authentication and authorization.

Suggested:

ADMIN:
- generate assignments
- regenerate assignments
- view assignments

STAFF:
- generate assignments if authorized
- regenerate if authorized
- view assignments

INSTRUCTOR:
- view assignments for authorized exams
- cannot regenerate unless explicitly authorized

Backend authorization is mandatory.

==================================================
45. IDOR PROTECTION

Protect:

GET /api/exams/{exam_id}/assignments

POST /api/exams/{exam_id}/assignments/preview

POST /api/exams/{exam_id}/assignments

POST /api/exams/{exam_id}/assignments/regenerate

The backend must verify the authenticated user's access to the exam.

Do not trust exam IDs from the frontend.

==================================================
46. API DESIGN

Suggested endpoints:

GET
/api/exams/{exam_id}/assignments

POST
/api/exams/{exam_id}/assignments/preview

POST
/api/exams/{exam_id}/assignments

POST
/api/exams/{exam_id}/assignments/regenerate

GET
/api/exams/{exam_id}/assignments/summary

GET
/api/exams/{exam_id}/assignments/{assignment_id}

Follow existing API conventions.

Do not create unnecessary endpoints.

==================================================
47. PREVIEW API

The preview API must:

- read authoritative database state
- validate capacity
- generate deterministic assignments
- return preview
- NOT modify the database

The preview result may contain:

student
room
seat
assignment_order

but should not create permanent records.

==================================================
48. CONFIRM API

The confirm API must:

- re-read authoritative data
- revalidate
- generate assignments
- insert transactionally
- return summary

Do not accept a client-generated list of assignments as authoritative.

This prevents tampering.

==================================================
49. REGENERATION API

The regeneration endpoint must:

- verify permission
- verify existing assignments
- require explicit confirmation
- execute transactionally

Do not expose a destructive endpoint without authorization.

==================================================
50. RESPONSE MODELS

Use Pydantic response models.

Examples:

SeatAssignmentResponse
AssignmentPreviewResponse
AssignmentSummaryResponse
RoomAssignmentSummaryResponse

Do not return raw ORM models.

==================================================
51. DATABASE INDEXES

Add indexes for likely queries:

exam_id
student_id
room_id
seat_id

Also support:

unique(exam_id, student_id)
unique(exam_id, seat_id)

Do not add unnecessary indexes.

==================================================
52. DATABASE MIGRATION

Create an Alembic migration for the assignment table.

Do not modify old migrations unnecessarily.

Verify:

alembic upgrade head

on a fresh database.

==================================================
53. FRONTEND ROUTE

Create:

/app/exams/[id]/seating

This becomes the main seating administration page.

==================================================
54. SEATING PAGE

Display:

Exam information

Roster:
185

Selected rooms:
3

Usable seats:
220

