57. REGENERATION UI

Show a strong confirmation dialog.

Example:

WARNING

This will replace the current seating assignment
for this examination.

Current assignments: 185

This action must be explicitly confirmed.

[Cancel]
[Regenerate]

==================================================
58. ASSIGNMENT TABLE

Use a proper semantic table.

Columns:

Order
Student Number
Student Name
Building
Room
Seat

Allow:

- search
- pagination
- room filtering

Do not add unnecessary columns.

==================================================
59. PRIVACY

Student information is sensitive.

Only authorized users should see:

- student number
- student name
- assigned seat

Do not expose assignment data publicly.

Do not put student information into URLs.

Prefer opaque resource IDs.

==================================================
60. LOGGING

Do not log:

student names
student numbers
full assignment lists

Safe logs:

ASSIGNMENT_GENERATION_STARTED
exam_id
student_count
available_seat_count

ASSIGNMENT_GENERATION_COMPLETED
exam_id
assignment_count

ASSIGNMENT_GENERATION_FAILED
exam_id
safe error code

==================================================
61. AUDIT EVENTS

Create:

SEATING_PREVIEW_GENERATED
SEATING_ASSIGNMENT_CREATED
SEATING_ASSIGNMENT_REGENERATED

Audit information:

actor
exam
timestamp
action
safe summary

Do not log the full student-to-seat mapping in the audit log.

==================================================
62. NO AI

Absolutely do not introduce AI.

The assignment algorithm must be:

deterministic
testable
explainable
reproducible

No:

LLM
machine learning
random model
optimization service
external AI API

==================================================
63. TESTING — BASIC ASSIGNMENT

Test:

1. 5 students / 10 seats
2. 10 students / 10 seats
3. 10 students / 15 seats
4. insufficient capacity
5. zero usable seats

==================================================
64. TESTING — DETERMINISM

Run the same input twice.

Verify:

student 1 receives same room and seat
student 2 receives same room and seat
...

The entire mapping must be identical.

==================================================
65. TESTING — ORDERING

Verify student order:

100000001
100000002
100000003

produces the expected assignment order.

Verify room ordering.

Verify seat ordering.

==================================================
66. TESTING — INVALID DATA

Test:

- student not in roster
- seat not in room
- room not selected for exam
- unusable seat
- inactive room
- duplicate student
- duplicate seat

All must fail safely.

==================================================
67. TESTING — TRANSACTION

Test:

If assignment 50 fails:

assignment 1–49 must NOT remain in the database.

Verify rollback.

==================================================
68. TESTING — REGENERATION

Test:

1. Generate assignment version 1.
2. Regenerate.
3. Verify old assignment is replaced atomically.
4. Verify new assignment remains internally consistent.

==================================================
69. TESTING — AUTHORIZATION

Test:

- unauthenticated user
- unauthorized user
- instructor read access
- staff generation
- admin generation
- unauthorized regeneration

==================================================
70. TESTING — IDOR

Test that a user cannot access another exam's assignments simply by changing:

exam_id

in the URL.

==================================================
71. TESTING — DATABASE CONSTRAINTS

Verify:

duplicate exam/student assignment rejected

duplicate exam/seat assignment rejected

invalid foreign keys rejected

==================================================
72. FRONTEND TESTS

Test:

- seating page
- capacity display
- preview
- confirmation
- assignment table
- search
- room filtering
- regeneration confirmation
- error states

==================================================
73. ACCESSIBILITY

Ensure:

- accessible tables
- keyboard navigation
- confirmation dialogs
- accessible forms
- clear error messages
- visible focus states

==================================================
74. PERFORMANCE

The system should support university-scale exams.

Avoid:

- loading all students unnecessarily
- loading all assignments into browser
- N+1 queries

Use:

- server-side pagination
- efficient joins
- indexes
- batch insertion

Do not introduce Redis or another infrastructure component.

==================================================
75. BULK INSERT

Assignment generation may create hundreds or thousands of rows.

Use an efficient database insertion strategy.

Do not execute one database INSERT request per student if the ORM supports
safe bulk insertion.

Maintain transaction safety.

==================================================
76. FUTURE COMPATIBILITY

The assignment model must support future:

Phase 6:
Crowdmark integration

Phase 7:
Personalized exam generation

Phase 8:
Seating map generation
Signature list generation

Therefore assignment data must provide:

student
student number
exam
building
room
seat
assignment order

Do not hard-code future document-generation behavior into this phase.

==================================================
77. DOCUMENTATION

Update:

README.md
DATABASE.md
DEVELOPMENT.md
ARCHITECTURE.md

Document:

- assignment model
- algorithm
- ordering rules
- capacity rules
- regeneration behavior
- authorization
- API endpoints

Especially document:

Student ordering:
student_number ASC

Room ordering:
building_code + room_number

Seat ordering:
row + seat_number

==================================================
78. ALGORITHM DOCUMENTATION

Clearly document the algorithm.

Example:

1. Sort students by student number.
2. Sort selected rooms by building code and room number.
3. Retrieve usable seats.
4. Sort seats by physical row and seat number.
5. Flatten seats into deterministic order.
6. Assign students sequentially.
7. Validate the complete result.
8. Persist atomically.

This is intentionally simple.

